#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5110235-92c1-5a27-a99c-d71e2fe24850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061398} {:answer/id #uuid "ce568ea6-ae0b-5a67-87a9-94b73170df32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061399} {:answer/id #uuid "da1b6afb-a864-5662-b19c-7fa8166bccb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061400} {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66de25-cfef-5e04-98e5-09487adc4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3f4f87-07a3-561b-a1e5-5bea819e76c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061397}] :question/id #uuid "34e33e76-2a83-5368-8561-def1a916040d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19201" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f^{′}(x), g(x)$, and $g^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1} \\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de19756b-6c9e-563b-9cb1-5a0b90131f73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279610} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d98025-b7f2-5a95-9b83-f1927db5e6a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b310eff-a1c1-5674-b489-414dfca2158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342621" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =x-1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{3} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ x+1 & =3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\ x+1 & =3{x}^{2}-6x+3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-7x+2 & =0 \\\\ (3x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3},2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x-\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{x+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{\\frac{2+1}{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =1-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
